.TH DATE 1 "September 19, 2026" maux
.SH NAME
date \- print the date
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B date
.RI [ +format ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The current date is printed, on maux to the minute:
.BR "YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM" .
A single operand beginning with
.B +
selects a format instead.
.TP
.B %Y %y %m %d
Year, two-digit year, month and day.
.TP
.B %H %M %S
Hour, minute and seconds. Seconds are always 00.
.TP
.B %F %D %R %T
Equivalent to %Y-%m-%d, %m/%d/%y, %H:%M and %H:%M:%S.
.TP
.B %n %t %%
Newline, tab and a percent sign.
.PP
Other characters are copied literally. For example:
.PP
.B date +%Y%m%d
.PP
The command does not set the clock.
.SH FILES
/usr/man/DATE  this page
.SH "SEE ALSO"
touch(1), time(1), licenses(1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Exit status is 0 on success, 1 for an absent or unusable clock stamp, 2 for usage errors or unsupported format conversions.
.SH BUGS
ProDOS time has minute resolution. Weekday/month names and day-of-year conversions are not implemented. The legacy year window pivots at 78; this is not a general calendar interface.
